NAME
    tail — Display the end of a file, because sometimes the beginning
    is old news.

SYNOPSIS
    tail [OPTIONS] <file>

DESCRIPTION
    The tail command displays the last lines of a file.

    By default, it displays the last 10 lines. The number of lines can
    be changed using the -n option.

USAGE
    > tail <file>       # Displays the last 10 lines of the file.
    > tail -n 5 <file>  # Displays the last 5 lines of the file.
    > tail -n 20 <file> # Displays the last 20 lines of the file.

FUN FACTS
    - tail only cares about the end of the file.
    - The default is 10 lines, because the beginning has already had enough attention.
    - tail is particularly useful for checking logs and recent events.
    - Unlike a real tail, this one cannot wag.

EXAMPLES
    > tail notes.txt
        Displays the last 10 lines of notes.txt.

    > tail -n 5 notes.txt
        Displays the last 5 lines of notes.txt.

    > tail -n 25 /home/user/log.txt
        Displays the last 25 lines of log.txt.

SEE ALSO
    help(1), cat(1), head(1), less(1), man(1)

    Remember, when you only care about how things ended,
    tail has your back.

